Open the canary stop / ring-insert / dispatch patch through your normal review path. If you want, I can draft the PR description and the runbook entry "Fairness canary: late-tenant admission stall" as the next step.

Telemetry read

The previous noise came from clients issuing a generic immediate retry on 429, which collapsed every rejected request into the same wall-clock instant and produced a thundering herd against the noisy-tenant fixture. With the immediate-retry branch disabled, retries now slot into the Retry-After windows the server was already returning (1s / 2s / 3s), so they fan out across time instead of stacking. Three observations confirm the diagnosis was client-side, not server-side:

  - low-volume tenants still pick up the next usable slots, i.e. fairness is preserved under the new pattern;
  - eligible capacity stays busy, i.e. the spread is not starving the system, just removing the spike;
  - accepted-point, dropped-point, and restart checks remain clean, i.e. nothing downstream is taking collateral damage.

The server was already emitting correct Retry-After; if it weren't, the spread wouldn't have materialized.

Decision on ingest-edge

No server-side change is warranted from this run. The guardrails you and I have been holding the staged rollout against (fairness, Retry-After, active-limit, accepted-point, dropped-point, restart) are all green, and the fix landed in the right layer. Touching ingest-edge now would widen scope onto a system that is not the cause of the noise. Leave the staged rollout on its current plan; the noisy-tenant fixture continues to serve as the regression sentinel.

Pending evidence — client release

  - a signed SDK build with the immediate-retry branch removed and an explicit release note tying the change to Retry-After honoring;
  - rollout staging for the SDK itself (25 / 50 / 100 of affected tenants, mirroring the discipline already on the ingest-edge side), with the noisy-tenant customer explicitly in the early cohort;
  - guardrails parity gate for the new build — same stop criteria as the server rollout, run against the noisy-tenant fixture before each step;
  - external customer-preview contract covering what tenants on the old SDK will observe during the cutover (transient elevated 429s are expected and not actionable);
  - a documented rollback path: ability to re-enable the old immediate-retry behavior per tenant or fleet-wide if a downstream regression appears.

Pending evidence — post release

  - the Wes ingest-edge rollout watch stays open through the full SDK rollout, not closed at SDK ship;
  - per-tenant retry distribution telemetry confirming the old immediate-retry pattern is gone for upgraded tenants and not reintroduced via a different code path;
  - aggregated 429 and retry-storm metrics across the fleet compared to the pre-fix baseline, with the noisy-tenant cohort broken out;
  - continued cleanliness of accepted-point, dropped-point, and restart at every rollout step, plus active-limit utilization steady-state;
  - cross-tenant fairness re-checked at each step, since the fix changes the load shape on the shared path;
  - a closed-loop confirmation that the noisy-tenant fixture still passes after the SDK reaches 100%, so the sentinel is not silently stale.

